The Python binding loads the installed shared `libpancake` through `ctypes` and
presents a `Peer` in
[`bindings/python/pancake.py`](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/bindings/python/pancake.py).
Wire values map to native Python values (integers, strings, bytes, lists, and the
rest), so you rarely think about the FFI underneath.

## Async

The async surface is [`asyncio`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/asyncio.html). An
`AsyncConnection` runs the peer over asyncio
streams inside an `async with` block, you iterate events with
`async for event in conn`, and you await a result with
`await vat.send(ref, verb, args)`. One connection owns one peer and lives on one
task, so the peer calls never block the event loop; only the socket awaits.

## Actors

The actor layer is a `Vat` over `behavior(verb, args)` callables. A local actor is
an ordinary Python callable, and a leading symbol argument selects the method verb.

## Dogfood and example

```sh
zig build python-dogfood    # the Python consumer against libpancake
```

The full surface lives in the
[Python binding README](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/bindings/python/README.md),
and the
[rooms chat client](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/examples/apps/rooms/python/room_client.py)
builds a small capability-secure room on this surface.
